  "prompt_text": "This figure summarizes how heat and moisture conditions interact to create compound climate extremes for crops and how those extremes translate into yield effects. The center of the diagram is “compound extremes,” including hot–wet, hot–dry, and alternating wet/dry conditions, shown around a crop illustration. Arrows indicate that several heat–moisture drivers feed into these events: circulation patterns that make hot and dry conditions co-occur seasonally, higher atmospheric water demand from warm air, land–atmosphere feedbacks that amplify warming and soil drying, and rainfall changes, with daily precipitation extremes strengthened by warming.\n\nThe figure also emphasizes crop and canopy feedbacks. Enhanced transpiration can cool the crop canopy but deplete soil moisture, whereas reduced transpiration can conserve soil moisture while increasing heating of the air and canopy. These crop–atmosphere interactions feed back into the compound extremes.\n\nBelow, the diagram shows that compound extremes affect plants through interacting stress pathways, acclimation, and cross-tolerance, leading to crop-physiological responses such as changes in transpiration and altered root, leaf, or flower growth. These responses ultimately shape compound yield impacts, which may either worsen losses or partly compensate for stress. Color coding separates heat–moisture, physiological, and crop–atmosphere interactions.",
